      "intendedParagraphs": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "blockIndex": 0,
          "paragraphInBlock": 0,
          "text": "In the International System of Units, the law of electromagnetic induction can be used to establish the unit of magnetic flux.\nThis unit is called the weber."
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "blockIndex": 1,
          "paragraphInBlock": 0,
          "text": "The unit of induced electromotive force is the volt, and the unit of time is the second.\nTherefore, equation (12-4) gives the following definition of the weber:\n**If the magnetic flux through the area enclosed by a closed loop decreases uniformly to zero in 1 second and produces an induced electromotive force of 1 volt, the original flux is 1 weber."
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "blockIndex": 1,
          "paragraphInBlock": 1,
          "text": "1 weber = 1 volt·1 second.**"
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "blockIndex": 2,
          "paragraphInBlock": 0,
          "text": "Equation (12-1) gives a way to establish the unit of magnetic flux density."
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "blockIndex": 2,
          "paragraphInBlock": 1,
          "text": "For a uniform magnetic field, if the vector $\\vec{B}$ is perpendicular to a plane of area $S$, then $\\Phi=BS$."
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "blockIndex": 2,
          "paragraphInBlock": 2,
          "text": "Therefore, if the magnetic flux through an area of 1 metre$^2$ is 1 weber, the magnitude of the magnetic flux density, measured in teslas, is 1."
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "blockIndex": 3,
          "paragraphInBlock": 0,
          "text": "As mentioned earlier, the unit of magnetic flux density is called the tesla.\n1 tesla = 1 weber/metre$^2$."
        }
      ],
      "resolvedBreakIndices": [
        3
      ],
